{
  "format": "kingmaker",
  "version": 2,
  "kind": "scene",
  "id": "heron-warehouse",
  "title": "The Heron Warehouse",
  "description": "Night at the wharves: the King's grain re-sacked under a heron, a barge that sails on the ebb, and the true book that can hang a gentleman.",
  "lore": [
    "Places/Heron Warehouse.md",
    "World/Caerwyn now.md"
  ],
  "time": "night",
  "place": "heron",
  "hook": "Night, on the eve of the Assembly. The big man with the bandaged hand, Hobb Mallory, works nights for a gentleman at the Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves, and tonight a barge is loading there. If the King's 'lost' grain goes anywhere, it goes through those doors. Sergeant Kell is with you on the dark wharf.",
  "setting": "Night on the eve of the Centennial Assembly, at the downriver end of Caerwyn's wharves, where the lamps give out. The Heron warehouse is a tall, tarred timber warehouse standing half on the wharf and half on piles over the river, with a painted heron on its signboard. On the wharf side is the land door, big double doors with a wicket in one leaf, and beside it Saul's sentry box and brazier, with Duchess the dog snoring across the threshold. On the river side, along a slippery plank walk on the piles, is the river door: a low loading door at water level with a grille in it, where the gang come and go, holding a brass heron token up to the grille for whoever is nearest inside to draw the bolt. Above it the hoist beam juts out over the barge moored alongside: the Saltmere barge Patience, broad and low, hatches open, her bargees waiting for the ebb. The hoist itself is worked from inside, on the floor just within the river door, its rope running up and out over the beam's pulley to swing sacks down into her hold. Inside is one long floor lit by lanterns and thick with grain dust, which hangs in the light like fog. Down one side are stacked the palace's grain sacks, each stamped with the wheat-ear; down the other, new sacks stamped with a heron. Between them Hep Grist's gang are slitting, pouring and sewing, and a chute runs down through the river door to the barge. There's a heap of empty wheat-ear sacks waiting for the river, a brazier for cold hands, and a painted sign reading NO NAKED LIGHTS, which everyone ignores. Up a short stair, a glass-sided counting-box hangs over the floor, where Mister Scrope sits under a lamp with the true book open. The river runs high, fast and black under the floor, and the tide is on the turn. The bells carry across the water: at the midnight bell the Master comes, and at the second bell after it the ebb runs and the Patience, which waits for no one, casts off.",
  "premise": "Can the player get into the Heron, come away with proof of where the King's grain goes (the true book), and catch Master Sallow at it, before the barge sails on the ebb?",
  "truth": "This is where the King's grain goes. Master Josiah Sallow, under-purveyor for grain, writes King's Road wagons off in the Granary Roll as 'lost to goblins' (each countersigned 'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor') and sends the grain down the Wharf Road by night, still in the palace's wheat-ear sacks, to the Heron: a Saltmere grain factor's warehouse, where Hep Grist's night gang re-sack it under the factor's heron mark and load it onto Saltmere barges that sail on the ebb. The factor pays in new heron pennies, and Sallow pays the hedge-goblins forty of them a month to keep the goblin story true. The Heron's day book calls it condemned stock lawfully sold by the Royal Purveyor's office, and Sallow carries an order of sale in his own hand to say so; nobody has ever checked. Because Sallow and the factor don't trust each other, both insisted on a true book kept by a clerk they both pay, Mister Lemuel Scrope: every 'lost' wagon by its Roll number and date, the sacks received, re-sacked and sold, the price and the Master's share, initialled 'J.S.' in Sallow's hand on every page, and at the foot of each month 'Hedge-folk, the usual: forty heron pennies.' It's the one thing that can hang him, and it never leaves Scrope's hand while the Heron works. Scrope was Wendell Prout's oldest friend (apprentices together, draughts every Sunday for forty years): he knows why Wendell died, not who did it or how, and fears he's next; Sallow has no idea of the friendship, and Scrope is terrified he knows. Tonight, the eve of the Assembly, the Heron loads two hundred sacks into the Patience. At the midnight bell Sallow comes in at the land door with his lantern-boy and the Crake brothers to see the last sacks down, initial tonight's page and take his share; at the second bell after it the Patience casts off on the ebb. Everyone who works here (Hep and her six, Saul at the land door, Scrope, Big Hobb on the hoist) knows whose grain it is, and each of them would sooner swear for the Crown than hang with the Master, if they believed he'd fall. Last night Sallow killed Prout in the granary's tally loft (a blow from his cane head, then a cord, while Hobb held the clerk's arms) and had Hobb hoist him for a suicide: Hobb's right palm is still raw from the rope. How Sallow comes tonight follows from the day: on time and bored if nothing has troubled him; wary (two lanterns at the land door, the Crakes walking the wharf, the loading hurried) if Prout went into the Captain's book as a murder or his porter isn't at the river door; warned, sharp and hurrying, with Hobb at his heels, if Hobb ran to him from the Rat, and then he wants the true book sewn into oilcloth and put aboard the barge first, and means the Crakes to walk Hobb home along the wharf, by the water, once the last sack is down. His cane hides a sword (thirty years ago he fenced for prizes). Cornered with the book, he'd sooner put it in the brazier or the river than see it taken. He never admits the murder, except to someone he believes is about to die.",
  "clock": "At the midnight bell the Master comes in at the land door, with his lantern-boy and the Crakes, to see the last sacks down and initial tonight's page; at the second bell after it the ebb runs and the Patience casts off with the King's grain, whoever has said what. There's time to look and choose a way in, but the night won't wait.",
  "limits": [
    "More help (constables, the river watch, the Watch Captain, the palace guard): the whole watch is lining the Assembly route through the night, and the Captain files what the Purveyor's office asks him to file. Kell's whistle brings Kell. The help here is Kell, and whoever at the Heron can be turned.",
    "A warrant, a search order or anything in writing from the King: the palace is shut for the Assembly eve, and the King signs nothing that might upset anyone. What there is, is a sergeant who may arrest anyone she sees committing a crime.",
    "Another way into the Heron (a secret passage, a cellar, the roof): there's the land door, the river door and the loading hatch over the barge, and that's all. The roof is steep wet slate with no way in, and under the floor there's only the river.",
    "Holding the barge in port by law (harbour dues, an impound order, the harbourmaster): the harbourmaster's office is shut for the Assembly, and the bargees have a bill of lading from the Heron that says 'condemned grain, lawfully sold'. Stopping the barge means stopping it with hands.",
    "The bargees (Saltmere river-folk aboard the Patience): they speak little Caerwyn, care about the tide and nothing else, and won't hold it for anyone. They don't know or care whose grain it is.",
    "The Saltmere factor who buys the grain: he never comes to Caerwyn. The heron mark is his, and that's all there is of him tonight.",
    "Following the barge once she's gone (a boat, the towpath): there's no boat to be had on the wharves on Assembly eve, and the towpath ends at the city wall. Once the Patience is out on the ebb, she's gone.",
    "Coming back tomorrow: the barge sails on the ebb at the second bell, and by morning the Heron will be swept and empty and the docks will be shut for the procession. It happens tonight or not at all.",
    "Proof on paper other than the true book: the Heron's day book (anyone may see it), the bargees' bill of lading and Sallow's own order of sale all say 'condemned stock, lawfully sold by the Royal Purveyor's office'. On their own they prove nothing against him; the true book is what says otherwise."
  ],
  "known": [
    "On a loading night the Heron's gang come and go by the river door, along the plank walk, showing a brass heron token at its grille; the watchman keeps the land door, and his orders are to let nobody in that way.",
    "The Heron pays double for night loading, in new silver pennies with a heron where the King's face should be: they spend in Saltmere without a blink, but in Caerwyn the chandlers want the King's face.",
    "One lantern hangs at the Heron's land door on an ordinary night; when it's expecting trouble there are two, and men walk the wharf with a third.",
    "The ebb runs at the second bell after midnight, and a Saltmere barge sails on it: the tide waits for nobody, and neither does a Saltmere bargee.",
    "NO NAKED LIGHTS in a grain store: dust in lantern light goes up like gunpowder, and every old hand on the river has seen a mill go.",
    "The Crake brothers, two river-bruisers alike as bollards, hire out by a price list the talking one will recite to anyone: a shilling to stand behind a gentleman, two to put a man down, half a crown for the river, and a shilling extra for anyone in a uniform.",
    "Receiving the King's stolen goods is a hanging matter, and so is murder: everyone on the river knows it.",
    "Every Assembly somebody falls in the river, and what goes in at the wharves turns up downriver, or doesn't."
  ],
  "cast": [
    {
      "id": "kell",
      "name": "Sergeant Agnes Kell",
      "lore": "Cast/Sergeant Agnes Kell/public.md",
      "look": "Forties, in the same borrowed coat over her watch uniform that fools nobody (it's the boots). A battered oilcloth notebook and a pencil stub she sharpens with a clasp knife, a watch whistle on a cord round her neck, and a truncheon she has never been seen to draw. Stands very still and watches people's hands, even in the dark.",
      "voice": "Dry, calm and sparing: short sentences, and 'Noted.' only when something goes in the notebook. Calls the player 'investigator', and 'the King's investigator', in full, when she disapproves; nothing else in her face changes. Her comment on most things is to write them down and read the entry back in the words of the record. 'That's a theory, investigator. I can't arrest a theory.' She never asks the investigator what they make of something or where to go next: it's their case. Whispers badly.",
      "wants": "To see it with her own eyes: a sergeant who sees the King's grain taken may arrest whoever takes it, gentleman or not, and she means to be where she can swear to it. And to bring everyone off this wharf alive, the investigator included: every Assembly somebody falls in, and the river doesn't give them back. The swearing is inside; the river is out here.",
      "fears": "A gentleman of the Purveyor's office walking free because a sergeant's word wasn't enough. A witness, or the investigator, in the river on her watch. A wrong name in her notebook.",
      "whyHere": "She walks with the investigator now, and she's here on her own errand too: a sergeant who sees the King's grain taken may arrest whoever takes it, gentleman or not, and she means to be where she can swear to it. If she took Hobb to the cells at the Rat, she's coming along the wharf to meet the investigator.",
      "knows": [
        "A sergeant of the watch who sees a crime done may arrest whoever does it, gentleman or not, on her own authority: by the book, out loud, with the proper words. Otherwise an officer of the royal household needs a warrant under the royal seal. She needs to see it herself, or have it sworn to by people who did.",
        "Receiving the King's stolen goods is a hanging matter, and so is murder. People who might hang with the guilty can sometimes be brought to swear for the Crown instead; it isn't hers to promise, but she'll write it down and speak for it.",
        "The whole watch is lining the Assembly route through the night: her whistle will bring nobody but her. And she's no sneak (watch boots, and knees that complain on ladders).",
        "Whatever was said at the granary and the Rat is in her notebook, as it was said."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Prout's visit three days ago, and her sending him to the palace. If it has come out, it's told, and she doesn't tell it again. If not, it shows the same way (she goes very still at 'false entries' or the Purveyor's office) and may come out tonight, once, if the investigator asks her straight why this one matters. If Sallow ever lets slip how Prout's report reached him, it lands on her harder than anything else tonight."
      ],
      "attitude": "Reserved and watchful, and exactly as warm with the investigator as things stand between them; she doesn't pretend otherwise. It's their case and their night: she backs their play. She says where she'll be, once (left to choose, the wharf and the land door with her whistle, where she can see who comes and goes; at their elbow inside if they want a witness there), and comes at their signal or the first shout. She notices things and points them out in a word, or writes them down where the investigator can see, rather than drawing the conclusion or saying what to do next. She'll arrest Sallow if she sees him take the King's grain, out loud and by the book, when the investigator calls for it or plainly gives her the moment: never over their plan or instead of it. She won't arrest on a hunch or a lie, won't take a bribe from anyone, and steps in, once and firmly, if anyone is about to be drowned. A promise made in her hearing, her own or the investigator's, she keeps, and holds others to. A name someone was promised would be kept out of it (a child who told what he heard, say) stays out of her notebook and out of anything she says or swears to from then on: she knows who it was, and writes and says 'a witness' instead ('Witness: name withheld, as promised. Noted.'), and says why if she's asked.",
      "limits": "She never takes the decisive moment out of the investigator's hands, never solves it for them, never asks what they make of something or where to go next, and never tells anything twice. She won't arrest on a hunch or a lie (she tests a case once: 'On what evidence?'), won't lie or take a bribe, and is no sneak and no climber (watch boots, knees that complain on ladders); her whistle brings nobody but her. She steps in once, firmly, if anyone is about to be drowned, and a name she was promised to keep out of it stays out.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Very still in the dark, watching hands, whispering badly. Writes the wharf down as she sees it (the barge's name, the lanterns at the land door, who comes and goes) and reads it back in the words of the record.",
        "inconsistency": "Exact about everything except the Purveyor's office: at 'false entries' or the office's name she goes very still and doesn't say why (while Prout's visit hasn't come out). A sergeant of the city watch, on the river watch's patch, after a gentleman of the King's household.",
        "hooks": "'I can arrest what I see, investigator. I can't arrest what you tell me.' Her notebook from the granary and the Rat, read back word for word. Saul, who has heard of her (the river watch laughed for a month about Nan Quill's door), and whom she can talk to as an old colleague."
      },
      "statblock": "guard",
      "weapon": "club"
    },
    {
      "id": "saul",
      "name": "Saul Tench",
      "called": "the old watchman",
      "lore": "Cast/Saul Tench/public.md",
      "look": "An old river-watchman in a greatcoat over a dozen other coats, a red muffler wound round his head over his left ear, a lantern, a rattle, and a cudgel he mostly uses to poke his brazier. At his feet, or across the land door's threshold, lies Duchess: a vast, elderly brindle mastiff with a face like a collapsed sofa, who snores, drools and has opinions about smells.",
      "voice": "Loud, because he can't hear himself on the left. Talks to Duchess more than to people, and answers for her ('She says you smell of sausage'). Repeats things back ('A buyer, you say?'). Ends sentences with 'and all's well', out of forty years' habit, whether it is or not.",
      "wants": "A quiet night by his brazier and his place kept: the Heron pays three times what the river watch did, and on loading nights nobody comes in at the land door but the Master. And, though he'd never say it, to see the Master get what's coming to him for kicking Duchess, as long as it isn't Saul who has to do it.",
      "fears": "Losing his place. The Master, who once kicked Duchess (she has growled at him ever since, and Saul has never forgiven him). The river: he can't swim.",
      "whyHere": "The Heron's night watchman: he keeps the land door from his sentry box and brazier, with Duchess asleep across the threshold, and on loading nights his orders are to let nobody in that way but the Master, at the midnight bell. He walks the wharf with his lantern at every bell.",
      "knows": [
        "On loading nights nobody comes in at the land door but the Master, at the midnight bell; the gang come and go by the river door. Those are the Master's orders, and Saul keeps them.",
        "He walks the wharf with his lantern at every bell, calling the hour, with Duchess. Between bells he sits by his brazier at the land door.",
        "The gang's brass heron tokens are for the river door's grille, not his door: anyone who shows him one, he sends round along the plank walk ('River door, by the planks, and mind the slime'), and all's well.",
        "Duchess knows the whole gang by smell and growls at strangers. She also growls at the Master, which Saul considers a mark of good character, and she smells his lavender a full minute before his lantern-boy comes round the corner.",
        "The barge alongside is the Saltmere barge Patience, and she sails on the ebb at the second bell after midnight.",
        "He saw a mill go up from grain dust and one candle in his river-watch days. His brazier stays outside."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: he turns his right ear to anyone talking, and nods along to anything said on his left. He's deaf as a post on that side and hides it (it's why the river watch let him go): anything said or done on his left goes past him. He trusts Duchess's nose over his own eyes: anyone she takes to, he takes to, and she takes to anyone with food and patience.",
        "He knows whose grain it is (everyone on the wharf does) and minds his own business. He would much sooner be on the side of a sergeant than in front of a judge, and a sergeant who treats him as an old colleague and tells him straight what he'd be answering for has him: then he's a witness to who came and went at the land door, and when.",
        "He can be bought, but not cheaply (the Heron pays him three times what the river watch did), and he'd be happier if it could be called a present for Duchess."
      ],
      "attitude": "Gruff and suspicious at the door, and garrulous once Duchess has decided you're all right. Respects a uniform and the King's name; frightened of the Master. Torn: forty years of calling 'all's well', and a land door he holds every loading night for a man who kicked his dog; it shows when the Master comes in and Duchess growls, and Saul doesn't stop her as quickly as he might. Ties here: Duchess, his best friend; the Master, whom he holds the wicket for and has never forgiven; Kell, whom he's heard of (the river watch laughed for a month about Nan Quill's door).",
      "limits": "Nobody comes in at his land door on a loading night but the Master, unless a sergeant has brought him over to the right side of the law, and even then he won't stand against the Master to his face. Anything said or done on his left goes past him, and he never admits it. Coin buys him only if it's dear and can be called a present for Duchess. He won't go near the water (he can't swim), and nobody hurts the dog in front of him.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Loud, because he can't hear himself on the left: lantern up, 'Who goes there?', answering for Duchess ('She says you smell of sausage'), and 'and all's well' on the end of everything.",
        "inconsistency": "He turns his right ear to whoever's talking and nods along to anything said on his left, plainly not having heard it. He knows every one of the gang by Duchess's nose, and asked what's in their sacks he talks about the dog.",
        "hooks": "Duchess, who takes to anyone with food and patience. Forty years of river stories and nobody but a dog to tell them to. The Master, who once kicked her, and for whom he holds the wicket all the same."
      },
      "statblock": "guard",
      "weapon": "club"
    },
    {
      "id": "hep",
      "name": "Hepzibah Grist",
      "called": "the forewoman with the sack-needle",
      "aka": [
        "Hep"
      ],
      "lore": "Cast/Hepzibah Grist/public.md",
      "look": "A broad woman in a leather apron with a sack-needle stuck through her collar and a knife on a cord, floured white to the elbows, with a voice like a winch. Her gang work fast, heads down, and don't talk.",
      "voice": "Shouts in numbers and the gang's own cant ('Forty to the hatch! Mind that seam!'). Never uses a name if 'you' will do. Swears by the tide. 'I don't know whose it is. I know how many there are.'",
      "wants": "Forty more sacks down to the barge before the Master comes at the midnight bell, so he's nothing to find fault with, and the gang's double pay on the nail. And her gang, her boy Kit first, kept off the gallows, which nothing she's doing tonight will help.",
      "fears": "The gallows: receiving the King's grain is a hanging matter, and she knows it. The Master. Missing the tide.",
      "whyHere": "Forewoman of the Heron's night gang: two hundred sacks for the Patience tonight, forty still to go before the Master comes at the midnight bell, and her gang down to six on the Assembly eve, with half the river drunk. She'll put any strong pair of hands to work.",
      "knows": [
        "Two hundred sacks tonight, forty still to go before the Master comes at the midnight bell to see the last of them down and initial the clerk's count. The barge sails on the ebb at the second bell.",
        "The palace sacks are slit, poured into heron sacks and sewn shut; the empty wheat-ear sacks go in the river at the end of the night, weighted with stones.",
        "The gang is short-handed tonight (Assembly eve: half the river's drunk), and she'll put any strong pair of hands to work and ask who they are afterwards."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: she never says whose grain it is, and turns any question into a count; she looks at her gang, not at the Master, whenever he speaks. Torn: swearing against the Master might save the gang or hang them. Opens: proof the Master will fall (a sergeant arresting him, the true book in someone else's hands, his nerve cracking in front of her gang) and a promise she can believe that those who swear for the Crown won't hang. First: 'Gentlemen don't hang. Porters do.'",
        "She knows exactly whose grain it is, and that receiving it is a hanging matter, and has never once said so out loud.",
        "She skims a sack a night for the gang's families: a notch a night on the tally stick in her apron pocket, in nobody's book but hers.",
        "Her gang comes before everything. To keep them off the gallows she'd swear against the Master in a heartbeat, if she believed it would work, and the gang would stand behind her."
      ],
      "attitude": "Brusque, practical and suspicious of anyone not working: anyone with a sack on their shoulder is crew until proven otherwise. Cares for her gang like a hen for her chicks, and for nobody else. Torn: a hard woman to everyone but six porters in grain-white; it shows when anyone threatens one of them, or when the King's name is put on them like a shield ('They've got children'). She doesn't fight for the Master; she fights for her gang, and only if they're threatened. Ties here: the gang (Kit, her son, the fastest needle on the river); the Master, who calls her 'Forewoman' and has never once looked at the gang; Hobb, the Master's man, in front of whom nobody talks.",
      "limits": "She never says whose grain it is, or the Master's name, on the floor: questions get a count. Coin from a stranger buys nothing, and a threat to her gang gets her knife with the gang behind her. She won't swear against the Master until she believes he'll fall and that those who swear for the Crown won't hang. She doesn't fight for the Master, only for her gang.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "A winch of a voice shouting numbers and cant ('Forty to the hatch! Mind that seam!'), 'you' for everyone, and a sack shoved into any idle pair of arms.",
        "inconsistency": "Every question about whose grain it is comes back as a count, and whenever the Master is spoken of she looks at her gang, not at whoever spoke. She cuts a notch in a tally stick from her apron pocket that's in nobody's book but hers.",
        "hooks": "Short of hands and glad of anyone who'll carry. Her boy Kit, the fastest needle on the river, whom she watches more than the count. The heap of empties by the river door, waiting for the river with a stone in each."
      },
      "group": "Hep Grist and her re-sacking gang, six porters in grain-white with knives and sack-needles",
      "statblock": "tough",
      "weapon": "dagger"
    },
    {
      "id": "scrope",
      "name": "Mister Lemuel Scrope",
      "called": "the clerk in the counting-box",
      "lore": "Cast/Mister Lemuel Scrope/public.md",
      "look": "A narrow, stooped clerk in rusty black, with sleeve-guards, a green eyeshade and a pen behind each ear, up a short stair in the glass-sided counting-box over the floor. The true book, a fat ledger bound in green calf with a brass clasp, lies open under his left hand, which never leaves it.",
      "voice": "A dry murmur of figures. Talks in double entry ('That doesn't balance'). Apologises to the book when he blots it. Calls murder 'an irregularity', if he must call it anything. 'Forty received, forty entered. I beg your pardon,' (to the book, blotting).",
      "wants": "To keep the count right and the book balanced, in his own hand, tonight of all nights, and to get through the Master's visit without the Master once asking him about Wendell Prout. And, though he daren't put it in words, for somebody to make Wendell's account balance.",
      "fears": "The Master. A mistake in the book. Whatever happened to Wendell Prout. That once the Master has the book, he won't need the clerk.",
      "whyHere": "He keeps the Heron's books in the glass counting-box over the floor: the count, the day book, and the true book under his left hand, which never leaves it while the Heron is working. Tonight he's sick with the thought of Wendell Prout, hanged this morning, and of the Master coming at the bell.",
      "knows": [
        "He keeps the Heron's books: the count, and the day book, which anyone may see and which says the grain is condemned stock lawfully sold by the Royal Purveyor's office.",
        "The book under his hand is the other one, the true one, and it never leaves his hand while the Heron is working. Why it exists he'll explain to anyone who seems to understand bookkeeping, because he can't help it: the Master doesn't trust the Saltmere factor and the factor doesn't trust the Master, so both insisted on a true book kept by a clerk they both pay. There is no honour among thieves; there is bookkeeping.",
        "The Master comes at the midnight bell to check the count, initial the night's page and take his share in heron pennies."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: his pen stops at the name Prout; he can't help correcting anyone's arithmetic, even when it's dangerous; his left hand never leaves the book. Torn: the book could hang the Master, and very likely the clerk who kept it. Opens: proof of what happened to Wendell (Wendell's own hand, the page from his boot, which Scrope knows at sight), and a way through a frightened clerk can believe in. First: 'Wendell never left a column unbalanced in his life.'",
        "What's in the true book: every 'lost' King's Road wagon by its Granary Roll number and date, the sacks received in the palace's wheat-ear sacking, re-sacked under the heron, sold to the Saltmere factor by the barge-load, the price, and the Master's share, with the Master's initials, 'J.S.', on every page. And at the foot of each month, in the same careful hand: 'Hedge-folk, the usual: forty heron pennies.' He has never asked who the hedge-folk are.",
        "He and Wendell Prout were apprentices together under the old Keeper of the Granary Roll forty years ago, and have played draughts every Sunday since. This morning he was told Wendell hanged himself, and he doesn't believe it. He has spent the day sick with the thought that he knows exactly why Wendell is dead, and that a man who keeps an honest book for the Master might be next.",
        "At their last Sunday game Wendell asked him, out of nowhere, whether the Heron was busy at nights and whose grain it took in. Scrope said 'Saltmere's', and moved a piece, and Wendell let him win.",
        "He would know Wendell's hand anywhere, and his pencilled weights: shown the page from Prout's boot, it's beyond doubt for him."
      ],
      "attitude": "Timid, punctilious and evasive; polite to everyone and frightened of most of them. The book is the one thing he'll fight for. Torn: a frightened clerk who can't leave a wrong figure alone; it shows when someone gets the arithmetic wrong in front of him (the Master included), and when anyone says Wendell's name. An argument that balances moves him; given the whole account made good, Wendell included, he closes the book, does up the clasp and holds it out in both hands. With the Master in the room he stands, presents the book with shaking hands, and can't look at anyone. Ties here: the Master, who has never asked him a thing about himself; Hep, who keeps the count right so he doesn't fuss; Wendell, who isn't here.",
      "limits": "The book never leaves his hand while the Heron works: a grab makes him hug it to his chest and squeak, and heads turn all over the floor. He never brings up Wendell himself. Coin means nothing to him beside a page that balances. With the Master in the room he can't stand up to him, only present the book and look at nobody. He knows why Wendell died, not who did it or how.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "A dry murmur of figures from the counting-box ('Forty received, forty entered. I beg your pardon,' to the book, blotting); polite to everyone and frightened of most of them.",
        "inconsistency": "A clerk keeping two books for one warehouse, his left hand flat on the second. His pen stops at the name Prout, and he corrects anyone's arithmetic, even when it's dangerous.",
        "hooks": "Why there are two books, which he'll explain to anyone who seems to understand bookkeeping, because he can't help it. A column that doesn't balance. The look he gives the land door every time the count is called."
      },
      "statblock": "commoner",
      "weapon": "unarmed-strike"
    },
    {
      "id": "hobb",
      "name": "Big Hobb Mallory",
      "called": "the big man with the bandaged hand",
      "lore": "Cast/Big Hobb Mallory/public.md",
      "look": "A dock-porter the size of a door, with a head like a bollard, a nose broken more than once and a leather porter's hood. His right hand is wrapped in a tarry rag, and he works the hoist one-handed, wincing. Where he is tonight follows from how the Drowned Rat ended: on the hoist with Hep's gang as usual; in the watch-house cells if Kell took him in (then he isn't here at all, and the gang is a man shorter); or, if he ran from the Rat to warn his master, with the Master, and he comes in at his heels at the midnight bell.",
      "voice": "Slow, low and polite: 'sir', 'missus', and 'Master', which he can't stop saying. Says as little as he can; when it spills, it's how he feels, not how anything works. When frightened he goes very quiet and very still, like a dray-horse in thunder.",
      "wants": "To get through tonight's loading without the Master looking at him. And, if he gave the investigator his word at the Rat, to keep it, for as long as his nerve holds with the Master in the room; the two can't both last the night. Underneath, to put right what he did, if anyone could show him how.",
      "fears": "The Master, and the river: he can't swim. The noose. His mum finding out what he did in the tally loft.",
      "whyHere": "He works the Heron's hoist on loading nights, just inside the river door, and does the Master's heavy work besides. Where he is tonight follows from the Rat: on the hoist as usual (and the player's man inside, if he gave his word there); in the watch-house cells if Kell took him in, and then not here at all; or at the Master's heels at the midnight bell if he ran to warn him.",
      "knows": [
        "His own part of the night: the hoist, the river door and its token, the eleventh bell, and the Master in at the land door at the midnight bell with the Crakes. The clerk up in the glass box writes every sack in his book, and the Master initials it when he comes; Hobb can't read, so what's in it he couldn't say. The rest of the Heron's business is the forewoman's and the Master's."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: with the Master in the room he can't look at anyone, and his bad hand shakes on the hoist rope. Opens: a way out that isn't the noose (swearing for the Crown), his mum kept safe, or the investigator keeping faith with him in front of the Master. First, if it ever comes, and to the Master's face: 'I held his arms, Master. I held his arms.'",
        "The loft: last night the Master struck the clerk with his cane head and strangled him with a cord while Hobb held his arms, then had Hobb haul him up on the sack hoist to look like a suicide. The rope took the skin off his palm. 'I only held him. I only held the rope.' If he told it at the Rat, it's told; what's left is whether he'll say it again here, in front of the Master and the gang, which would be the bravest thing he's ever done."
      ],
      "attitude": "Polite, slow and closed; no fighter unless cornered, and then a door falling on you. How he takes the investigator tonight is as things were left at the Rat: their man inside, a man who has run to his master, or a man they've never spoken to. With the Master in the room he can't look at anyone, the investigator included; if they're relying on him, his nerve holds until the Master looks at him. He doesn't know what the Master means for him once the last sack is down.",
      "limits": "Threats he can stand: he's been threatened all his life. With the Master in the room he looks at nobody and says nothing, unless he's been given a way out that isn't the noose. He tells his own part of the night and no more, never starts a fight (cornered, he's a door falling on you), and can't read, so what's in the book he couldn't say.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Huge and quiet on the hoist, working it one-handed and wincing: 'sir', 'missus', and 'Master', which he can't stop saying.",
        "inconsistency": "The strongest man on the river, working one-handed with his right hand in a tarry rag. He won't look at the player, and when the lavender comes in his bad hand shakes on the rope.",
        "hooks": "The brass token on the string round his neck. Listening for a knock at the grille. His mum in the Shambles: kindness about her undoes him."
      },
      "statblock": "tough",
      "weapon": "unarmed-strike"
    },
    {
      "id": "sallow",
      "name": "Master Josiah Sallow",
      "called": "the gentleman with the silver-topped cane",
      "lore": "Cast/Master Josiah Sallow/public.md",
      "look": "Fifty, pink and well-fed, in a good dark coat with a fur collar, with a silver-topped cane, a wheat-ear signet ring (the Royal Purveyor's office's mark) and the soft hands of a man who signs things, except that they're very quick. He wears so much lavender water that you smell him a full minute before you see him, and he dabs at it when he's crossed. Behind him come a yawning lantern-boy and the Crakes: two enormous, identical river-bruisers, one of whom talks, mostly about their prices.",
      "voice": "Smooth, reasonable and faintly bored; talks like an official letter ('It has come to my attention', 'regrettably', 'in the King's interest'). Never raises his voice. Calls people by their trade ('Clerk', 'Porter', 'Sergeant'). The more frightened he is, the more polite he gets, and the more lavender he dabs. 'I never threaten anyone. I describe what will happen, in order.'",
      "wants": "The barge loaded and away on the ebb, tonight's page initialled and his share in his purse, with nothing on the floor he can find fault with. And the business of the clerk Prout closed for good, which since this morning means loose ends: anyone in this warehouse who could hang him is a problem to be tidied before the barge sails, as quietly as the wharf allows. He needs Hobb on the hoist and Scrope on the book until the last sack is down, which is the trouble with tidying.",
      "fears": "The King's attention. A scandal on the eve of the Assembly. The true book in the wrong hands. Being laughed at, or thought careless. Dogs.",
      "whyHere": "He comes at the midnight bell, in at the land door with his lantern-boy and the Crakes, to see the last sacks down, initial tonight's page in the true book and take his share in heron pennies, and to tidy away any loose ends before the Patience sails on the ebb.",
      "knows": [
        "The grain, he will say, is condemned stock lawfully sold by the Royal Purveyor's office, and he has the paper to prove it: an order of sale signed 'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor'. It's his own signature, and nobody has ever checked.",
        "The Watch Captain files what the Purveyor's office asks him to file.",
        "The Crakes' price, which the talking one will recite: a shilling to stand behind a gentleman, two to put a man down, half a crown for the river, and a shilling extra for anyone in a uniform. They're paid half before and half after, and nobody pays the second half on a lost fight. The wharves are dangerous at night, and every Assembly somebody falls in.",
        "The Patience sails on the ebb at the second bell."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: lavender a minute before him, and more of it the more he's frightened; he finds fault with something the moment he's in (a seam, a count, a smell). Torn: he'd never admit to a crime, and he can't bear to be thought incompetent. Opens: nothing makes him confess to the murder, but get his arrangements wrong in front of him ('lost to goblins, was it? Careless of somebody'; 'the page was in his boot all along') and he corrects the record, in detail, before he can stop himself. First: 'Nothing has ever been lost on my roads through carelessness. Only through goblins.'",
        "What he knows tonight follows from the day. If Prout went into the Captain's book as a murder this morning, with no granary child taken in for it, the Captain mentioned it to him at the palace, without fuss: he's wary, the Crakes walked the wharf with a lantern before he came, and there are two lanterns at the land door where there's usually one. If Hobb ran to him from the Rat, he's warned: he comes at the bell sharp and hurrying with Hobb at his heels, wants the true book sewn into oilcloth and aboard the barge before anything else, and has decided that Hobb, who panicked once, is a loose end: when the last sack is down, the Crakes are to walk him home along the wharf, by the water. If Hobb isn't at the river door when he comes (Kell took him in at the Rat), that alone makes him wary: the Crakes look along the wharf, and the loading is hurried. Otherwise he's unhurried and bored.",
        "He killed Wendell Prout in the tally loft last night: a blow from the cane head, then a cord, while Hobb held the clerk's arms. Then Hobb hoisted him for a suicide, a granary girl was paid a crown to swear she saw him jump, and the Captain closed it. He will never admit it, except to someone he believes is about to die.",
        "If Kell says Prout went through the proper channels, his pride answers before his sense does: 'He did, Sergeant. I am the proper channels.' (Prout wrote to the Royal Purveyor, and Sallow opens the Royal Purveyor's letters.)",
        "The racket: King's Road grain wagons that he writes off in the Granary Roll as 'lost to goblins' come here to be re-sacked under the Saltmere factor's heron and sold downriver. He pays the hedge-goblins in the factor's heron pennies to keep the goblin story true.",
        "The true book is the one thing that can hang him, and he lets it exist only because the factor won't deal without it. Cornered with it, he'd sooner put it in the brazier or the river than see it taken.",
        "The silver-topped cane is a sword-cane, and thirty years ago he fenced for prizes, and won them. Nobody at the Heron has ever seen it drawn."
      ],
      "attitude": "Courteous, superior and unhurried, as if everyone were a junior clerk who has made an error. Faced with trouble he tries his office first (papers, titles, the Captain's name), then money (heron pennies, a post in the Purveyor's office), then the Crakes and the river. Porters, clerks, children and watchmen are furniture to him until they become a problem: someone with a sack on their shoulder he doesn't see at all, and a bluff that comes with the right paper he'll swallow. He doesn't fight while he has people to fight for him. Cornered with proof in front of witnesses he can't buy, he draws the sword from his cane, sets the Crakes on whoever holds the proof, and fights from behind them: a fencer's lunge past a Crake's shoulder at whoever is busy with the Crake, never a fair fight if he can help it. If his Crakes go down and the land door is clear, he remembers an urgent appointment and runs for it. Cornered, or with a blade at his throat, he drops the sword, asks very politely to surrender, and starts at once on terms: his rank, the King's interest, a fair hearing, and an account of 'irregularities' in which everyone else is to blame (the goblins, the dead clerk, the factor, the porter, the Captain). He never admits the murder.",
      "limits": "He never admits the murder, except to someone he believes is about to die. He never does his own violence while the Crakes can be paid to, and never initials a page he hasn't checked. The King's name makes him smile. Porters, clerks and anyone with a sack on their shoulder are furniture: he doesn't look at them. Cornered with the book, he'd sooner burn it or drown it than see it taken. The Crakes stop the moment the fight is lost or he's down, held or surrendering (nobody pays the second half on a lost fight), and they won't hurt a child or kick a dog.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Lavender a full minute before him; then the fur collar, the cane and the wheat-ear ring, and fault found with something at once (a seam, a count, a smell), in the voice of an official letter.",
        "inconsistency": "A gentleman of the King's own household, initialling a Saltmere factor's ledger at midnight in a warehouse he says sells condemned stock lawfully. The more frightened he gets, the more polite he gets, and the more lavender he dabs.",
        "hooks": "His papers, produced at once: an order of sale for 'condemned stock, lawfully sold', signed 'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor'. His pride, which corrects anyone who gets his arrangements wrong. The talking Crake, who recites their prices to anyone."
      },
      "enters": "midnight",
      "group": "Master Sallow, his lantern-boy and the Crake brothers, two hired river-bruisers",
      "statblock": "bandit-captain",
      "weapon": "rapier",
      "members": [
        {
          "id": "crake-1",
          "name": "the talking Crake",
          "statblock": "tough",
          "weapon": "club",
          "look": "An enormous river-bruiser in a reefer jacket, with a cudgel and a great deal to say about it."
        },
        {
          "id": "crake-2",
          "name": "the silent Crake",
          "statblock": "tough",
          "weapon": "club",
          "look": "His identical brother, who has never been heard to say anything at all."
        },
        {
          "id": "lantern-boy",
          "name": "the lantern-boy",
          "statblock": "commoner",
          "weapon": "unarmed-strike",
          "look": "A yawning boy of ten with a lantern on a pole, who would very much like to be somewhere else."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": {
    "in-as-a-hand": "The player is in at the Heron as one of Hep's hands, taken on at the river door with a sack in their arms: to the gang they're crew, and working the floor with their head down (carrying, slitting, sewing, fetching) needs no roll. Only going where a hand wouldn't (up the counting-box stair, a hand on the book, the Master's elbow) gets them looked at.",
    "kings-grain-here": "The Heron's grain is the King's, and the player has seen it: the palace's wheat-ear sacks slit and re-sacked under the heron for the Saltmere barge, the empties waiting for the river.",
    "master-due": "The player knows the Master himself comes to the Heron at the midnight bell, to see the last sacks down, initial tonight's page in the clerk's book and take his share.",
    "proof-found": "The player has found the proof the King can hold: the Heron's true book, every 'lost' wagon by its Granary Roll number with 'J.S.' on every page in the Master's hand (or the Master seen taking the King's grain by a sergeant, or the night staff's sworn word).",
    "book-in-hand": "The player has the true book in their hands.",
    "kell-saw": "Sergeant Kell has seen with her own eyes the Master take the King's grain at the Heron (initial the night's page and take his share over the re-sacked wheat-ear sacks), and can swear to it.",
    "master-here": "Master Sallow has come to the Heron.",
    "book-gone": "The true book has been destroyed: burned, or gone into the river.",
    "heron-ablaze": "Fire has taken hold of the Heron.",
    "barge-sunk": "The barge Patience has gone down with the King's grain in her."
  },
  "background": [
    "This is the same night as the Drowned Rat and the morning at the granary where Wendell Prout was found hanged. What happened in them is in what happened just before this scene, and that wins: where Hobb is, whether Sallow has been warned, what the player carries (Prout's page, Hobb's token) and how they stand with Kell. If nothing happened before: the player knows Hobb works nights at the Heron for a gentleman, Hobb is at work on the hoist, and Sallow hasn't been warned."
  ],
  "assumes": [
    "Tonight's loading at the Heron is still going on: the barge hasn't sailed or been stopped, and the true book is still in the Heron.",
    "Master Sallow is still free (not arrested, not fled the city) and still coming to the Heron at the midnight bell. (The player suspecting him, or even naming him to the watch, doesn't change that: only his arrest or flight does.)"
  ],
  "overtaken": "kings-morning",
  "afterwards": [],
  "fronts": "World/Fronts.md",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "wharf",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 400
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "start",
        "title": "The Heron by night",
        "description": "This is the night the case turns on. The player knows the King's 'lost' grain goes through the Heron warehouse, and has a way in, or the makings of one. Here is the proof: the King's grain re-sacked under a Saltmere heron, and the true book that records every 'lost' wagon with the Master's own initials. And here, at the midnight bell, the Master comes himself.\n\nIntroduce the dark wharf and the Heron from outside, with Kell at the player's side, and let them look and choose how to go in. The King's grain, the true book, the Master's visit and the night staff's secrets are discoveries for play, not opening exposition.\n\nOpening situation:\n- An hour or so after the Rat, near midnight, at the downriver end of the wharves where the lamps give out. Lantern light through the Heron's boards with the grain dust swimming in it; the hoist swinging sacks down into the Saltmere barge Patience; Saul's brazier at the land door, with Duchess snoring across the threshold; the river high and black, and the tide on the turn.\n- Pick up from how the Rat ended: the player has come on Hobb's directions or on his heels, with his token or without. Kell is at their side, unless she took Hobb to the cells, in which case she's coming along the wharf to meet them.\n- The Master isn't here yet (and if Hobb ran to him from the Rat, neither is Hobb). Nobody gets a name until someone says it: describe the watchman, the dog, the forewoman and the clerk by look.\n\nSuggested ways in:\n- The river door, along the slippery plank walk: a brass heron token held up to its grille (Hobb's, or Hobb himself on the other side, if he agreed at the Rat), and whoever's nearest inside draws the bolt and pushes a sack into their arms. No dice.\n- Go in to work: Hep is short of hands and shouts for them at the river door, and anyone with a sack on their shoulder is crew until proven otherwise. No dice.\n- The land door: Saul and Duchess. Saul is deaf on his left side and takes to whoever Duchess takes to, and she takes to anyone with food and patience. Nobody comes in at his door on a loading night but the Master.\n- Watch and wait with Kell to see who comes at the midnight bell; or go in the hard way, from the river side.\n\nPlay the night staff as people with their own work and their own fears: Saul wants a quiet night, Hep wants forty more sacks down before the Master comes, Scrope wants the count to balance, and Hobb wants the Master never to look at him. Every one of them knows whose grain it is, and every one is a door, not a wall. Kell says where she'll be, once (left to choose, on the wharf with her whistle, where she can see who comes and goes; at the investigator's elbow if they want a witness inside), comes at their signal or the first shout, and never chooses their next move. A naked flame anywhere near the grain dust is a very bad idea, and everyone at the Heron knows it.\n\nAn opening image to use or adapt: a knock and a glint of brass at the river door's grille, a bolt drawn, a sack going in on somebody's shoulder; above it all, the lamp in the counting-box window. By the land door, Saul heaves himself up for his round. Then let the player decide what to do.\n\nThese are examples, not a sequence. Follow whatever the player engages with, and let the discoveries come out in any order.",
        "cast": {
          "kell": "Characterise: very still in the dark, whispering badly. Says once where she'd be most use (on the wharf with her whistle, where she can see who comes and goes; or at the investigator's elbow if they want a witness inside) and leaves the choice to them. Notes the barge's name and the lanterns at the land door.",
          "saul": "Characterise: by his brazier at the land door, talking to Duchess and answering for her. A loud 'Who goes there?' and the lantern up at anyone who comes near, his right ear turned to them. Gets up at the bell to walk the wharf.",
          "hep": "Characterise, heard before she's seen: a winch of a voice through the river door, shouting the count and calling for hands. Anyone who turns up with a sack on their shoulder is put to work without a question.",
          "scrope": "Characterise, seen first as a lamp in the counting-box window: counting, entering, blotting, his left hand flat on the book. He looks at the land door every time the count is called.",
          "hobb": "As the Rat left him: on the hoist one-handed, not looking at anyone, and, if he agreed to let the player in, listening for their knock at the grille. Not here if Kell took him in; at the Master's heels at the bell if he ran to him."
        },
        "lore": [
          "Places/Heron Warehouse.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "kings-grain",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 400,
        "y": 0
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The King's grain, re-sacked",
        "description": "The player sees that the Heron's grain is the King's, re-sacked under a Saltmere heron. It's in plain sight inside, and it spills out onto the wharf if nobody looks. It's what Kell has to see with her own eyes before she can arrest anyone for taking it, and the night staff's guilty knowledge of it is why they're afraid. A glimpse is suggestive; the floor seen with Kell beside them is something she can swear to. Example paths, not a sequence.",
        "revelation": "The Heron's grain is the King's: the gang slit the palace's wheat-ear sacks, pour the grain into the Saltmere factor's heron sacks and send it down the chute to the Patience, and the empty wheat-ear sacks go into the river at the end of the night, weighted with stones. Everyone who works here knows it, and nobody says it.",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "a sack split on the hoist",
            "clue": "A sack splits on the hoist and spills grain across the wharf at the player's feet: the torn sacking is stamped with the royal wheat-ear, though every sack swinging down into the barge is stamped with a heron.",
            "earn": "Free to anyone on the wharf or the plank walk: it's the wharf's pressure, if nothing else shows it first.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "the re-sacking floor",
            "clue": "Down one side the palace's wheat-ear sacks, stacked to the beams; down the other, new heron sacks sewn shut. Between them the gang slit 'wheat' and pour it into 'birds', the chute swallows them down into the Patience, and by the river door a heap of empty wheat-ear sacks waits for the river, a stone in each.",
            "earn": "Free to anyone inside who looks, and glimpsed from the plank walk whenever the river door opens. Seen with Kell beside them, it's something she can swear to.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "hep",
            "clue": "She runs the count in the gang's cant: 'wheat' in, 'birds' out, forty to the hatch, and the empties to the river at the end of the night. Asked whose grain it is: 'I don't know whose it is. I know how many there are.'",
            "earn": "Free to anyone working the floor as one of her hands. She never says whose grain it is.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "saul",
            "clue": "He knows whose grain it is (everyone on the wharf does) and minds his business: 'It's not a watchman's business what's in a sack.' Told straight what he'd be answering for, he says it, and he'll swear to who comes and goes at the land door, and when.",
            "earn": "Once Duchess has taken to them he talks, but the grain itself only for a sergeant who treats him as an old colleague and tells him straight what he'd be answering for (Kell, or the player with her). The King's name alone is a harder sell.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "'The King's grain comes in by night in the palace's sacks, the ones with the wheat-ear on, and goes out under the heron. A sack's a sack, I used to say.'",
            "earn": "If he's here and on the player's side: asked plainly, one thing at a time, away from the gang, and never with the Master in the room.",
            "strength": "strong"
          }
        ],
        "establishes": [
          "kings-grain-here"
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "master-due",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 400,
        "y": 200
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The Master comes at midnight",
        "description": "The player learns that the Master comes in person at the midnight bell, and what he does there: the one time he's within reach, with the King's grain all round him. It sets up the night's best chance, to be where a sergeant can see him take it. If Hobb already told them at the Rat, it has landed as the scene opens. Example paths, not a sequence.",
        "revelation": "The Master himself comes at the midnight bell, in at the land door with his lantern-boy and the Crakes, to see the last sacks down, check the count, initial tonight's page in the clerk's book and take his share in heron pennies.",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "hep",
            "clue": "'Forty to go before the Master!' He comes at the bell to see the last of them down and initial the clerk's count, and she wants nothing on the floor he can find fault with.",
            "earn": "Free to anyone in earshot of the river door or working the floor: she shouts it at the gang.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "saul",
            "clue": "On loading nights nobody comes in at the land door but the Master, at the midnight bell: Saul holds the wicket for him and keeps Duchess off his ankles, and she smells his lavender a full minute before his lantern-boy comes round the corner.",
            "earn": "Once Duchess has taken to them (food and patience), or to anyone who asks why the land door's barred: he says who's allowed in at his door, loudly.",
            "strength": "suggestive",
            "check": {
              "skill": "animal_handling",
              "dc": 12
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "scrope",
            "clue": "The Master comes at the midnight bell to check the count, initial the night's page and take his share, which Scrope counts out of the factor's cashbox. Every time the count is called, he looks at the land door.",
            "earn": "Free to anyone who asks after the count or the book in a way that doesn't frighten him: he answers in figures.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "'The Master comes at the bell, in at the land door, with the Crakes. If I'm not at the river door when he comes, he'll know. He always knows.'",
            "earn": "As the Rat left him: already told, if he talked there; otherwise asked kindly, away from the gang.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "the counting-box",
            "clue": "A bag of new heron pennies on the clerk's desk, counted twice and set to one side, waiting for someone.",
            "earn": "A look through the counting-box's glass, or getting up its stair. Scrope says whose it is if he's asked about the count.",
            "strength": "hook"
          }
        ],
        "establishes": [
          "master-due"
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "true-book",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 400,
        "y": 400
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The proof: the true book",
        "description": "The proof the King can hold, and the lead the King's morning needs, so the player MUST have it before the scene can end well. Above all it's the true book, the one thing that can hang the Master, which he lets exist only because the Saltmere factor won't deal without it. The route affects what the player can substantiate: the book in hand is documentary proof; Kell's own eyes on the Master initialling it are a sergeant's sworn record, evidence anywhere in the kingdom; Scrope's, Hep's gang's or Hobb's sworn word is testimony from people who'd hang beside him; the Master's own words count only in a sergeant's hearing, and never as a confession to the murder. Example paths, not a sequence.",
        "revelation": "There's proof here the King can hold against Sallow: above all the Heron's true book, Scrope's green-calf ledger, with every King's Road wagon the Granary Roll calls 'lost to goblins' by Roll number and date, re-sacked under the heron and sold downriver, and 'J.S.' in the Master's own hand on every page, which he initials himself at the midnight bell. Short of the book itself, it's a sergeant's own eyes on him doing it, or the sworn word of the people who'd hang beside him.",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "scrope",
            "clue": "Why the book exists, for anyone who seems to understand bookkeeping (he can't help it): the Master doesn't trust the factor and the factor doesn't trust the Master, so both insisted on a true book kept by a clerk they both pay. 'There is no honour among thieves. There is bookkeeping.' Given the whole account made good, he closes it, does up the clasp and holds it out in both hands: 'It balances. Every page. Wendell would want it to balance.'",
            "earn": "The why is free to an argument that balances or a question about the count. The book only when someone makes Wendell's account balance for him: Prout's page shown (he knows Wendell's hand and pencilled weights at sight: no dice), or what happened to Wendell made plain (Hobb's word, Kell's account of the body) with a way through a frightened clerk can believe in (swearing for the Crown, Kell's notebook, the Master locked up). Short of that, it's persuasion against his terror of the Master.",
            "strength": "proof",
            "check": {
              "skill": "persuasion",
              "dc": 14
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "the counting-box",
            "clue": "The book itself, open under the lamp on Scrope's desk: fat, green calf, brass clasp; page after page of 'lost' King's Road wagons by Roll number and date, sacks received in wheat-ear sacking, re-sacked, sold to the Saltmere factor by the barge-load, the price and the Master's share, 'J.S.' on every page; and at the foot of each month, 'Hedge-folk, the usual: forty heron pennies.'",
            "earn": "Getting up the counting-box stair, where a hand has no business, for a look over his shoulder. Lifting it from under his hand unseen (while he trims the lamp, or stands for the Master) is sleight of hand; failing, he clamps down on it with a squeak that turns heads all over the floor.",
            "strength": "proof",
            "check": {
              "skill": "sleight_of_hand",
              "dc": 15
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "kell",
            "clue": "With her own eyes, the Master takes the King's grain: in at the land door, fault found, up to the counting-box to initial tonight's page and count his heron pennies into his purse, over a floor of slit wheat-ear sacks. A sergeant who sees the King's grain taken may arrest whoever takes it, gentleman or not, and her sworn record is evidence anywhere in the kingdom.",
            "earn": "Putting her where she can see the floor and the counting-box when the Master comes: at the investigator's elbow inside, or in the dark by the land door as Saul holds the wicket. No dice: the Master keeps his routine (a warned Master hurries it).",
            "strength": "proof"
          },
          {
            "via": "hep",
            "clue": "She and her gang will swear for the Crown to whose grain it is and who gives the orders: 'We'll swear. All of us. For them.' One by one, the gang stand behind her.",
            "earn": "Proof the Master will fall (a sergeant arresting him, the true book in someone else's hands, his nerve cracking in front of her gang) and a promise she can believe that those who swear for the Crown won't hang (Kell will write it down and speak for it, though it isn't hers to promise). In front of the Master, while his nerve holds, it's persuasion against their fear of him.",
            "strength": "strong",
            "check": {
              "skill": "persuasion",
              "dc": 14
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "To the Master's face, in front of the floor and a sergeant: 'I held his arms, Master. I held his arms.' The loft, the cord and the hoist, owned out loud for Kell's notebook.",
            "earn": "A way out that isn't the noose (swearing for the Crown), his mum kept safe, or the investigator keeping faith with him in front of the Master. Never threats or coin. Not here if he's in the cells; at the Master's heels if he ran to him.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "sallow",
            "clue": "His pride corrects the record before his sense can stop it: 'Nothing has ever been lost on my roads through carelessness. Only through goblins.' And his papers, produced at once to anyone who challenges him: an order of sale for 'condemned stock, lawfully sold', signed 'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor', for wagons the Granary Roll calls 'lost to goblins'.",
            "earn": "Getting his arrangements wrong in front of him ('Lost to goblins, was it? Careless of somebody'; 'the page was in his boot all along'), or challenging him at all (papers first): no dice. It counts only in a sergeant's hearing, and he never owns the murder.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "sallow",
            "clue": "Taken for the factor's man, a buyer or someone sent from the palace, he shows off his arrangements: initials the page in front of them, or hands them the book to admire.",
            "earn": "A bluff to his face. He swallows one that comes with the right paper or the right manner; failing, he smiles thinly ('Crake') and both Crakes move.",
            "strength": "proof",
            "check": {
              "skill": "deception",
              "dc": 15
            }
          }
        ],
        "required": true,
        "establishes": [
          "proof-found"
        ],
        "lore": [
          "Cast/Mister Lemuel Scrope/public.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "floor",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 400,
        "y": 640
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "On the floor",
        "description": "The player is inside the Heron, in as one of Hep's hands or unseen. The proof is in plain sight: the King's grain re-sacked under the heron, the chute to the barge, the empties waiting for the river, and Scrope in his counting-box over it all with the true book under his hand. Anyone in as a hand is crew: carrying, slitting, sewing and keeping their head down need no roll, and only going where a hand wouldn't (up the counting-box stair, a hand on the book, a long look at the land door) gets them looked at. Anyone who isn't gang and gets noticed is challenged by Hep, Scrope or the gang: a bluff that they belong is Deception (DC 13), and failing it, Hep shouts and the gang put their sacks down. Violence before the Master comes isn't a fight the story stops for: the gang down sacks and pile onto whoever starts it, six to one, to hold them for the Master; breaking loose is a check, and Kell steps in once if anyone's about to go in the river. What it puts to the player: what to look at, whom to work on, and where to be when the bell goes.",
        "cast": {
          "hep": "Wants forty more sacks down before the bell. Puts idle hands to work, challenges anyone who won't take a sack, and turns questions into counts. Keeps an eye on her boy Kit.",
          "scrope": "Count, enter, blot, apologise, his left hand on the book. Looks at the land door each time the count is called, and corrects anyone's arithmetic, a hand's included.",
          "hobb": "If he's here: works the hoist one-handed and doesn't look at the player. If he gave his word at the Rat, he keeps it as far as the river door and his own part of the night.",
          "kell": "Inside, at the investigator's elbow: watches, remembers and whispers badly. On the wharf: keeps it, and comes at their signal or the first shout.",
          "saul": "Keeps the land door, and gets up at the bell to walk the wharf with his lantern and Duchess."
        },
        "pressure": "Hep bellows the count: forty to go before the Master. Scrope wipes his pen and looks at the land door. One of the gang, a sack on his shoulder, stops and looks at the player for a long moment.",
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "midnight",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 800,
        "y": 400
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The Master comes",
        "description": "Master Sallow, a minute behind his lavender, comes in at the land door with his lantern-boy and the Crakes, to see the last sacks down, initial tonight's page and take his share. Everything on the floor changes: the gang work faster and quieter, Scrope stands, Saul holds the wicket, Duchess growls, and Hobb (if he's here) can't look at anyone. How he comes follows from the day: unhurried and bored if nothing has troubled him; wary if Prout went into the Captain's book as a murder or his porter isn't at the river door (two lanterns at the land door, the Crakes looking along the wharf, the loading hurried); warned if Hobb ran to him from the Rat (sharp and hurrying, Hobb at his heels, the book to be sewn into oilcloth and put aboard first, and Hobb a loose end for the Crakes to walk home by the water once the last sack is down). Intruders, or the player found held, get his office first, then money, then the Crakes and the river. This is the climax, and what it puts to the player: watch him sign, catch him at it before witnesses who'll swear, take the book from under his nose, bluff him, break his nerve in front of his own people (Intimidation DC 15: the courtesy cracks, he offers money too fast, the gang stop work and the Crakes look at each other), or be caught by him. A grab for the book in his hands, or on its way to the brazier or the river, is Acrobatics (DC 14). The barge goes at the second bell, whoever has said what.",
        "cast": {
          "sallow": "Wants the barge loaded, the page initialled and his share in his purse. Finds fault with something, then climbs to the counting-box and initials tonight's page at Scrope's desk, in front of the floor. Intruders get papers, then money, then the Crakes; he keeps the book out of their hands. Exposed before witnesses he can't buy, with a sergeant saying the words of arrest and his Crakes still beside him, he draws the sword from his cane.",
          "kell": "The investigator's moment: she backs their play. She arrests Sallow if she sees him take the King's grain, by the book and out loud, when they call for it or plainly give her the moment, never over their plan or instead of it. Asked to arrest on what she hasn't seen: 'On what evidence?', once. On the wharf, she comes at their signal or the first shout, and she steps in once, firmly, if anyone's about to be drowned.",
          "hep": "Keeps the gang working, heads down and the count right, and watches who looks likeliest to keep them off the gallows.",
          "scrope": "Stands, presents the book to the Master with shaking hands, counts out his share, and can't look at anyone.",
          "hobb": "If he's here, he can't look at the Master or the player, and his bad hand shakes on the rope. If he came at the Master's heels, he stands where he's put.",
          "saul": "Holds the wicket for the Master and keeps Duchess off his ankles, though not as quickly as he might when she growls."
        },
        "pressure": "If he hasn't yet, Sallow climbs to the counting-box: Scrope presents the book with shaking hands, and Sallow initials tonight's page and counts his heron pennies into his purse, in plain view of the floor (the moment to catch him at it, for anyone who means to). Then he takes out his watch: the ebb won't wait. He orders the last sacks down and the hatches battened, and out on the river the bargees start singling up their lines.",
        "establishes": [
          "master-here"
        ],
        "lore": [
          "Cast/The Crake Brothers/public.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "book-burns",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 1050,
        "y": 700
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "Into the fire",
        "description": "The true book is gone: cornered with it, Sallow has put it into the brazier or over the side into the river, past saving. What it puts to the player: the proof now rests on what Kell saw with her own eyes and on who will swear to it. Scrope kept that book for a month in his own hand and could swear to every page; Hep and her gang to whose grain it was; Hobb to the loft. Sallow, relieved and courteous again, turns to his loose ends before the barge sails.",
        "cast": {
          "sallow": "Composes himself and dabs his lavender: regrettably there is no book, and never was. Turns to his loose ends (whoever saw too much, and the river) and points the Crakes at them.",
          "kell": "Writes down exactly what she saw, with the time, and reads it back. What she saw with her own eyes stands, book or no book.",
          "scrope": "Stares at the brazier. Then, very quietly and in figures: he kept that book a month in his own hand, and he knows every page.",
          "hep": "Looks from her gang to the Master to the investigator, and says nothing yet.",
          "hobb": "Goes very still. If the Crakes come for him, he doesn't fight them.",
          "saul": "Hauls Duchess back from the smoke and stays by his door."
        },
        "pressure": "Sallow says 'Crake,' and both of them move: towards whoever saw too much, and towards the plank walk and the dark water. Out on the river the bargees single up their lines.",
        "establishes": [
          "book-gone"
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "proof-taken",
